<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?><ns2:section xmlns:ns2="http://rest.cis.illinois.edu" id="63847" href="schedule/2022/spring/CWL/220/63847.xml"><parents><calendarYear id="2022" href="schedule/2022.xml">2022</calendarYear><term id="120221" href="schedule/2022/spring.xml">Spring 2022</term><subject id="CWL" href="schedule/2022/spring/CWL.xml">Comparative and World Literature</subject><course id="220" href="schedule/2022/spring/CWL/220.xml">Origins of Western Literature</course></parents><sectionNumber>D  </sectionNumber><sectionTitle>Animals in Literature</sectionTitle><statusCode>A</statusCode><sectionText>A selection of literary texts from Greece, Rome and the subsequent Western tradition in which animals are protagonists or narrators. Readings from Plutarch, Ovid, Apuleius; Anna Sewell's Black Beauty; Jack London's Call of the Wild. We conclude by looking beyond the Western tradition to Native American texts, including Louise Erdrich's The Antelope Wife.</sectionText><partOfTerm>B</partOfTerm><sectionStatusCode>A</sectionStatusCode><enrollmentStatus>UNKNOWN</enrollmentStatus><startDate>2022-03-14Z</startDate><endDate>2022-05-04Z</endDate><meetings><meeting id="0"><type code="LCD">Lecture-Discussion</type><start>11:00 AM</start><end>12:50 PM</end><daysOfTheWeek>TR     </daysOfTheWeek><roomNumber>311</roomNumber><buildingName>Gregory Hall</buildingName><instructors><instructor lastName="Williams" firstName="C">Williams, C</instructor></instructors></meeting></meetings></ns2:section>